NOTABLE_GAPS:
Empty or thin cells: race, colonialism, gender, animals and disability in 2015 (no candidate cleared the significance rule; Storey on 'second thoughts' on race and Harfouch and Nale on racial mind-body unions were seen but carry few indicators); animals and disability in all three years (Korsgaard, 'Kantian Ethics, Animals, and the Law', 2013, was excluded as an extension of Kantian ethics rather than Kant interpretation); religion in 2014 (Michalson's Critical Guide and Pasternack's Routledge guidebook were screened out on indicators); the Opus postumum beyond Hall (2014); pre-critical work beyond Watkins (2013); education and pedagogy; Metaphysics of Morals and virtue in 2014 and 2015 (Surprenant 2014 screened out); science and mathematics in 2014 (Massimi on laws and Heis in the Canadian Journal of Philosophy special issue on Kant's mathematics were screened out to keep the block within its cap); no NDPR review was found for Friedman, Mensch, Insole, Dyck, Frierson's Empirical Psychology, Maliks, Goy and Watkins, Allison, O'Neill or Palmquist, so those rest on citations, journal reviews and SEP listings.
CONSIDERED_NOT_INCLUDED (with reasons): Formosa, EJP 21(2) 2013 (carry-forward; S2 26, CrossRef 16, not in the retrieved SEP bibliographies, below the included practical-philosophy entries); Heidemann (ed.), Kant and Non-Conceptual Content (year ambiguous: SEP kant-mind and PhilPapers 2012, S2 2013, CrossRef online 2014-06 with no print date; no NDPR review found); Kerstein, How to Treat Persons (2013) and Stern, Kantian Ethics (2015; about half the essays concern later reception), which are Kantian theory rather than Kant interpretation; Waxman (2013), Sweet (2013), Timmermann (AGPh 2013), Wood's Free Development of Each (2014; Kant and successors), Vinci (2014), Boehm (2014), Golob's Heidegger monograph (2014; Kant is not the principal interlocutor), Landy (2015), Rauscher (2015), Luft (2015), Gava and Stern (2015), Cavallar (2015), Schafer's Philosophy Compass surveys (2015) and Proops, Stang and McLear (2015), all screened out on indicators or on the per-year cap.
INCOMPLETE ENTRIES (no abstract field, flagged INCOMPLETE and no-abstract by the enrichment step; each SUMMARY is grounded in the source named by its summary-source tag): books: mensch2013organicism (University of Chicago Press description via web search), dyck2014rational (OUP page; the resolver's text was front matter and was dropped), beiser2014neokantianism (NDPR), allison2015deduction (OUP page), ferrarin2015powers (NDPR; the resolver's text was garbled and was dropped), gardner2015turn (NDPR; the resolver's text was a table of contents and was dropped), palmquist2015religion (Wiley description via web search); articles: ware2014sensibility (Project MUSE abstract via web search; the resolver's text was the article's opening and was dropped), mills2014race (journal page abstract via web search), mcnulty2015regulative (University of Minnesota Experts page abstract via web search). The enrichment script's NDPR fuzzy match attached reviews of other books to allison2015deduction (a review of a Heidegger lecture course) and palmquist2015religion (a review of a Kant translation); both were removed by hand. The NDPR abstracts kept (mikkelsen2013race, ginsborg2014normativity) were checked and match the books. DOI handling: nine entries (friedman2013construction, wuerth2013sense, matherne2013aesthetic, gomes2014naive, stang2014nonidentity, denis2014ethics, allais2015manifest, reath2015practical, ferrarin2015powers) carry the DOI in the url field rather than in a doi field, because the SubagentStop metadata_cleaner overwrites the year from the first DOI-matching API record, and for these DOIs S2 and CrossRef records disagree (online-first year, or an S2 record dated 2015-2017), which would move them across year blocks.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Year sections can be organised by the subfield and debate tags; cross-reference Marshall (2013), Stang (2014) and Allais (2015) for the two-aspect line, and Tolley (2013), Gomes (2014), Onof and Schulting (2015) and Stephenson (2015) for the perception line. Where an entry's YEAR NOTE records a conventional date one year later (Denis and Sensen, Ginsborg, Maliks, Palmquist), the CrossRef print year has been kept so that the year assignment is reproducible.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Two-aspect, one-world and non-phenomenalist readings of transcendental idealism: Marshall 2013, Stang 2014, Allais 2015.
- Nonconceptualism and naive realism about perception: Tolley 2013, Gomes 2014, Onof and Schulting 2015, Stephenson 2015.
- Empirical psychology, anthropology and the life sciences in Kant: Mensch 2013, Frierson 2013 and 2014, Cohen 2014, Goy and Watkins 2014, Hall 2014.
- Moral realism versus constructivism and moral feeling: Wuerth 2013 and 2014, Ware 2014, O'Neill 2015.
- Kant, race and colonialism: Mikkelsen 2013, Flikschuh and Ypi 2014, Mills 2014.
====================================================================
}

@book{friedman2013construction,
    author = {Friedman, Michael},
    title = {Kant's Construction of Nature: A Reading of the Metaphysical Foundations of Natural Science},
    publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
    year = {2013},
    url = {https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781139014083},
    note = {SUMMARY: Develops a new and complete reading of Kant's Metaphysical Foundations of Natural Science (1786), reconstructing its main argument in detail and explaining its relationship to Newton's Principia and to eighteenth-century scientific thinkers such as Euler and Lambert; by situating the text against Kant's pre-critical writings and the changes made to the second edition of the Critique of Pure Reason, it argues for a new perspective on the meaning and development of the critical philosophy as a whole (publisher abstract). SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Philosophy of science and the critical system: Kant's natural philosophy and its relation to Newtonian physics and to the first Critique. No single named debate; a book-length reading of the Metaphysical Foundations. SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations: 75 on the title-matched record (retrieved 2026-09-30; a second S2 record carrying the CUP DOI shows 19); CrossRef is-referenced-by-count: 108. SEP: listed in the kant, kant-leibniz, kant-reason and kant-science bibliographies. Journal review: Journal of the History of Philosophy (Sutherland, 2016; CrossRef). NDPR: no review found. Publisher: Cambridge University Press. YEAR NOTE: CrossRef print date 2013-01-17, online 2013-02.},
    keywords = {kant, tp-science-math, tp-cpr, type-monograph, High},
    abstract = {Kant's Metaphysical Foundations of Natural Science is one of the most difficult but also most important of Kant's works. Published in 1786 between the first (1781) and second (1787) editions of the Critique of Pure Reason, the Metaphysical Foundations occupies a central place in the development of Kant's philosophy, but has so far attracted relatively little attention compared with other works of Kant's critical period. Michael Friedman's book develops a new and complete reading of this work and reconstructs Kant's main argument clearly and in great detail, explaining its relationship to both Newton's Principia and eighteenth-century scientific thinkers such as Euler and Lambert. By situating Kant's text relative to his pre-critical writings on metaphysics and natural philosophy and, in particular, to the changes Kant made in the second edition of the Critique, Friedman articulates a radically new perspective on the meaning and development of the critical philosophy as a whole.},
    abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{tolley2013nonconceptuality,
    author = {Tolley, Clinton},
    title = {The Non-Conceptuality of the Content of Intuitions: A New Approach},
    journal = {Kantian Review},
    year = {2013},
    volume = {18},
    number = {1},
    pages = {107--136},
    doi = {10.1017/s1369415412000313},
    note = {SUMMARY: Argues that Kant takes intuitions to have content in the sense of Frege's Sinn (a mode of presentation of an object or property) and that this content is distinct in kind from the content of concepts. The paper holds that the conceptualism debate had stalled for lack of agreement on what 'content' means, and shows how this non-conceptualist account answers the most serious objections to earlier non-conceptualist readings (abstract). SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Theoretical philosophy: Kant's theory of intuition and mental content; conceptualism versus nonconceptualism. SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations: 75 (retrieved 2026-09-30); CrossRef: 40. Venue: Kantian Review. SEP: listed in the kant-judgment and kant-spacetime bibliographies. YEAR NOTE: CrossRef print date 2013-03, online 2013-02.},
    keywords = {kant, tp-mind, tp-cpr, debate-conceptualism, type-article, Medium},
    abstract = {Abstract There has been considerable recent debate about whether Kant's account of intuitions implies that their content is conceptual. This debate, however, has failed to make significant progress because of the absence of discussion, let alone consensus, as to the meaning of ‘content’ in this context. Here I try to move things forward by focusing on the kind of content associated with Frege's notion of ‘sense (Sinn)’, understood as a mode of presentation of some object or property. I argue, first, that Kant takes intuitions to have a content in this sense, and, secondly, that Kant clearly takes the content of intuitions, so understood, to be distinct in kind from that possessed by concepts. I then show how my account can respond to the most serious objections to previous non-conceptualist interpretations.},
    abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{marshall2013quaobjects,
    author = {Marshall, Colin},
    title = {Kant's Appearances and Things in Themselves as Qua-Objects},
    journal = {The Philosophical Quarterly},
    year = {2013},
    volume = {63},
    number = {252},
    pages = {520--545},
    doi = {10.1111/1467-9213.12060},
    note = {SUMMARY: Proposes understanding Kant's appearance/thing-in-itself distinction through an Aristotelian notion of a 'qua-object' (following Kit Fine), which gives a sense in which appearances and things in themselves can be the same things while differing in many of their features. The proposal is offered to the one-world interpretation of Kant's idealism, which faces problems arising from the different features Kant assigns to the two (abstract). SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Theoretical philosophy: transcendental idealism; one-world (two-aspect) readings against two-world readings of the appearance/thing-in-itself distinction. SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations: 16 (retrieved 2026-09-30); CrossRef: 13. Venue: The Philosophical Quarterly. SEP: listed in the kant-transcendental-idealism bibliography. YEAR NOTE: CrossRef print date 2013-07, online 2013-06.},
    keywords = {kant, tp-cpr, debate-two-aspect, type-article, Medium},
    abstract = {The one‐world interpretation of Kant's idealism holds that appearances and things in themselves are, in some sense, the same things. Yet this reading faces a number of problems, all arising from the different features Kant seems to assign to appearances and things in themselves. I propose a new way of understanding the appearance/thing in itself distinction via an Aristotelian notion that I call, following Kit Fine, a ‘qua‐object.’ Understanding appearances and things in themselves as qua‐objects provides a clear sense in which they can be the same things while differing in many of their features.},
    abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{watkins2013newtonianism,
    author = {Watkins, Eric},
    title = {The Early Kant's (Anti-) Newtonianism},
    journal = {Studies in History and Philosophy of Science Part A},
    year = {2013},
    volume = {44},
    number = {3},
    pages = {429--437},
    doi = {10.1016/j.shpsa.2012.10.015},
    note = {SUMMARY: Argues that the pre-critical Kant, besides using 'Newtonian principles' in the Universal Natural History, developed a major criticism of Newton based on subtle metaphysical issues about God, with implications for how the formation and constitution of the heavenly bodies should be scientifically explained. The abstract adds that Kant remained interested in these issues later, in the Critical period (abstract from CORE). SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Pre-critical work and philosophy of science: Kant's early relation to Newtonian physics and natural theology. Part of the special issue of Studies in History and Philosophy of Science 44(3) introduced by Massimi as 'Philosophy of natural science from Newton to Kant' (per CrossRef titles of the issue). SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations: 37 (retrieved 2026-09-30); CrossRef: 35. Venue: Studies in History and Philosophy of Science Part A. SEP: listed in the kant-science bibliography. The same issue carries papers by Stan, Schliesser, Smith, Massimi and De Bianchi, DiSalle and Domski (CrossRef). YEAR NOTE: CrossRef print date 2013-09 (issue 44(3)).},
    keywords = {kant, precritical, tp-science-math, type-article, Medium},
    abstract = {It is well known that during his pre-Critical period, Kant was a major proponent of Newtonian physics, for the project of the Universal Natural History explicitly uses "Newtonian principles" to explain the formation of the various bodies that constitute our solar system as well as those that lie beyond. What has not been widely noted, however, is that the early Kant also developed a major criticism of Newton, one that is based on subtle metaphysical issues pertaining to God, which are most at home in philosophical theology. Interestingly, this criticism is neither an inchoate precursor of his later criticisms of Newton's account of absolute space, nor isolated to the abstract realm of metaphysics, but has a wide range of implications for the way in which a scientific account of the formation and constitution of the heavenly bodies ought to be developed, that is, for the kind of argument Newton offered in the Principia. That Kant remained interested in this set of issues later in his Critical period suggests that, alongside the revolutionary changes that comprise transcendental idealism, there are deep continuities not only in his Newtonian commitments, but in his anti-Newtonian tendencies as well. © 2012 Elsevier Ltd},
    abstract_source = {core}
}

@book{grenberg2013common,
    author = {Grenberg, Jeanine},
    title = {Kant's Defense of Common Moral Experience: A Phenomenological Account},
    publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
    year = {2013},
    doi = {10.1017/cbo9781139520126},
    note = {SUMMARY: Argues that everything important in Kant's moral philosophy emerges from reflection on the common human experience of the conflict between happiness and morality. Through readings of the Groundwork and the Critique of Practical Reason she finds a phenomenological notion of experience in Kant's account of the Fact of Reason and gives feeling an epistemic role in grounding a priori morality, against readings that assign feeling only a motivational role and against Fichtean readings (publisher abstract; NDPR). SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Practical philosophy: Groundwork III and the Critique of Practical Reason; the Fact of Reason and the role of moral feeling. NDPR also reports a new interpretation of the failure of the Groundwork III argument for moral obligation. SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations: 44 (retrieved 2026-09-30); CrossRef: 46. NDPR review: yes (ndpr.nd.edu/reviews/kant-s-defense-of-common-moral-experience-a-phenomenological-account/). Other reviews: Journal of the History of Philosophy (Denis, 2015), Dialogue (Way, 2015), Kantian Review (Sticker, 2014). SEP: not found in the retrieved bibliographies. Publisher: Cambridge University Press. YEAR NOTE: CrossRef print date 2013-07-18, online 2013-07.},
    keywords = {kant, pp-ethics, type-monograph, Medium},
    abstract = {In this book, Jeanine Grenberg argues that everything important about Kant's moral philosophy emerges from careful reflection upon the common human moral experience of the conflict between happiness and morality. Through careful readings of both the Groundwork and the Critique of Practical Reason, Grenberg shows that Kant, typically thought to be an overly technical moral philosopher, in fact is a vigorous defender of the common person's first-personal encounter with moral demands. Grenberg uncovers a notion of phenomenological experience in Kant's account of the Fact of Reason, develops a new a reading of the Fact, and grants a moral epistemic role for feeling in grounding Kant's a priori morality. The book thus challenges readings which attribute only a motivational role to feeling; and Fichtean readings which violate Kant's commitments to the limits of reason. This study will be valuable to students and scholars engaged in Kant studies.},
    abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{wuerth2013sense,
    author = {Wuerth, Julian},
    title = {Sense and Sensibility in Kant's Practical Agent: Against the Intellectualism of Korsgaard and Sidgwick},
    journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
    year = {2013},
    volume = {21},
    number = {1},
    pages = {1--36},
    url = {https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-0378.2010.00432.x},
    note = {SUMMARY: Presents Kant's account of practical agency as embodied agency, drawing on his empirical-psychological and metaphysical descriptions, and argues against the intellectualized interpretations of Kant's practical agent offered by Korsgaard and Sidgwick. On this reading the agent chooses from a reflective distance but not from the complete affective distance of reason alone, and its sensibility (sense, feeling, desire) belongs to the agent that chooses (abstract). SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Practical philosophy: moral psychology and the theory of action; critique of intellectualist readings (Korsgaard, Sidgwick). Carry-forward from Domain 1 (EJP 21(1)). SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations: 19 (retrieved 2026-09-30); CrossRef: 24. Venue: European Journal of Philosophy. SEP: listed in the kant-mind bibliography. YEAR NOTE: CrossRef print date 2013-03 (volume 21, issue 1); online-first 2010-09-26, which is why S2 lists 2010.},
    keywords = {kant, pp-ethics, tp-mind, debate-other-intellectualism, type-article, Medium},
    abstract = {Abstract: Drawing on a wide range of Kant's recorded thought beyond his Groundwork for the Metaphysics of Morals, this essay presents an overview of Kant's account of practical agency as embodied practical agency and argues against the intellectualized interpretations of Kant's account of practical agency presented by Christine Korsgaard and Henry Sidgwick. In both Kant's empirical‐psychological and metaphysical descriptions of practical agency, he presents a recognizably human practical agent that is broader and deeper than the faculty of reason alone. This agent chooses action from a reflective distance, but not from the complete affective distance of reason. We choose neither as reason alone, nor even as the sum of all of our ‘higher’ faculties, of cognition (including reason), feeling, and desire, in Kant's view. Instead, we choose as an agent that also has ‘lower’, ‘sensible’ faculties, of cognition (including sense), feeling, and desire, which together comprise our ‘sensibility’. These mental states of sensibility are not merely confused versions of our higher cognitions, feelings, or desires, and so different from them in degree only, but are instead distinct from our higher states in kind also; and these distinct sensible states are not dependent on our higher states and so on a commitment to the value of humanity, for example. For this reason, a choice in favour of sensible states and in opposition to the moral law need in no way undermine its own foundations and thus be incoherent, as Korsgaard and Sidgwick argue, but instead only immoral. Because Kant does not reduce the problem of immoral choice to one of insufficient reflection and ensuing confusion, he does not view moral progress in cognitive terms alone, whether in the form of a clearer understanding of the moral law, improved judgment in applying it, or deeper insight into our own nature as practical agents. Kant instead recognizes the crucial importance of cultivating our feelings and desires over the course of a lifetime both as a way to make ourselves worthy of the humanity within us directly and also as a way to facilitate future moral choices.},
    abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{trampota2013tugendlehre,
    editor = {Trampota, Andreas and Sensen, Oliver and Timmermann, Jens},
    title = {Kant's ‘Tugendlehre’: A Comprehensive Commentary},
    publisher = {De Gruyter},
    year = {2013},
    doi = {10.1515/9783110229875},
    note = {SUMMARY: Running commentary on the Doctrine of Virtue (Tugendlehre) of the Metaphysics of Morals by 18 scholars from Germany, Italy, Britain and the United States, discussing topics such as suicide, truthfulness, moral perfection, beneficence, gratitude, sympathy, respect and friendship as well as Kant's moral psychology (publisher abstract). SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Practical philosophy: Metaphysics of Morals, Doctrine of Virtue; the abstract presents the volume as a response to recent interest in Kant's later ethical work. SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations: 72 (retrieved 2026-09-30); CrossRef: 9. SEP: a 'Kant's Tugendlehre: A Commentary' entry is listed in the kant-moral bibliography. Review: Kant-Studien (Wittwer, 2016; CrossRef). NDPR: no review found. Publisher: De Gruyter. YEAR NOTE: CrossRef print and online date 2013-01-18 (S2 also 2013); the SEP kant-moral bibliography dates its entry 2011.},
    keywords = {kant, pp-mm-virtue, type-edited-volume, Medium},
    abstract = {Building on the results of the Groundwork and the Critique of Practical Reason , Kant finally published his system of moral philosophy in two volumes in 1797. By then, he had been planning to write a Metaphysics of Morals for three decades; but only the title remained unchanged while the basic principles of his theoretical and practical philosophy changed dramatically. While for many years academic moral philosophy focused mainly on Kant’s earlier ethical treatises, there has recently been much interest in this later and perhaps more mature work on moral philosophy, particularly the ethical part of the Metaphysics of Morals, the “Metaphysical Principles of the Doctrine of Virtue” or “Tugendlehre”. The present volume responds to these demands. Following a series of research workshops, 18 scholars from Germany, Italy, Britain and the United States provide a seamless commentary on the “Doctrine of Virtue”, discussing topics such as suicide, truthfulness, moral perfection, beneficence, gratitude, sympathy, respect and friendship as well as Kant’s moral psychology, philosophy of action and theory of moral education. This book will be an invaluable resource for moral philosophers and Kant scholars alike.},
    abstract_source = {openalex},
}

